          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the difference between E11 and E11.9, and when is each the right thing to quote?",
          "answer": "In short: E11 is the parent category for type 2 diabetes, and E11.9 is one specific code within it. The sources I searched don't say when a coder should pick one over the other, so I can't give you that rule.\n\n**What the sources say**\n\n- **E11 is a family, not one code.** It is \"never used at three characters.\" The fourth and later characters name the complication, such as kidney involvement, retinal involvement, neuropathy, circulatory problems, skin or joint complications, or high or low blood sugar. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*, US ICD-10-CM educational summary.)\n- **E11.9 is the code for diabetes documented without complications.** Its final digit 9 marks that case. (Same source.)\n- **Insulin use does not change the E11 code.** A person taking insulin is still coded to E11, with long-term insulin use recorded as a separate status code. (Same source.)\n- **Type 2 has its own category.** The endocrine chapter gives type 1, type 2 and other diabetes each a separate three-character category. (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 4: endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases*.)\n\n**What the sources don't cover**\n\n- The rule for when a coder should choose E11.9 rather than a complication code.\n- How a coder decides that a complication is documented.\n- Current code validity. The sources note that the code set is revised each year, effective October 1, so check the version that applies to your case.\n\nFor a specific record, a trained medical coder or the official current ICD-10-CM tables are the right check.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
